**Corollary 8.4 (Pareto status and simultaneous sharp averages).** Every
vector in Theorem 8.1 is undominated in the full original twelve-cost
problem. In an exceptional segment, distinct parameters give incomparable
vectors. The equality set of any one original sharp mean is exactly the
equality set of all three sharp means.

Proof. Each displayed vector has four-sign average $g_y(s)$ at every
height, since $\sum_{e,n}en=0$. If another full-class design weakly
lowered every coordinate and strictly lowered at least one, its average
at that coordinate's height would be strictly below the universal sharp
mean, a contradiction. In an exceptional segment $t\kappa>0$ and
$\sqrt{y(64-y)}/128>0$ at every original height. Increasing $\theta$
strictly raises every $en=+1$ coordinate and strictly lowers every
$en=-1$ coordinate. Distinct segment vectors are therefore incomparable.
Necessity in Theorem 8.1 and sufficiency in Theorem 8.2 prove the last
assertion. This is a statement about twelve resource costs and their
three averages, not a unique full implementation. $\square$

